		// Basic Commands
:-> pwd: Displays your present/current working directory path.
:-> ls: Lists files and folders.
:-> cd: Changes your current directory.
:-> mkdir: Creates/Make a new direcory.
:-> touch: Creates a blank file.
:-> cat: Displays file content immediately.
:-> cp: Copies files or folders.
:-> mv: Moves or renames files.   //If move in same folder then it rename the file.
:-> rm: Deletes/remove files or folders.
:-> clear: Clears the screen/Terminal.

		//Date commands with flags.
:-> date: Displays current date, time, and timezone.
:-> date -u: Displays time in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC).
:-> date -R: Outputs date in RFC-2822 format for emails.
:-> date -I: Shows date in ISO-8601 standard format (YYYY-MM-DD).
:-> date -d "next Friday": Displays the date of the upcoming Friday.
:-> date -d "yesterday": Shows yesterday's exact date and time.
:-> date -d "10 days ago": Calculates the date from 10 days in the past.
:-> date +"%Y-%m-%d": Formats output strictly as Year-Month-Day.
:-> date +"%H:%M:%S": Formats output strictly as Hours:Minutes:Seconds.
:-> date +"%A": Displays only the full current weekday name.

		// Dispaly, Merge, Sort a file Commands 
:-> cat: filename: Outputs the entire file content all at once.
:-> cat > filename: Creates a new file and opens an empty prompt.  // ctrl + D to save file 
:-> cat >> filename: Appends new text to the bottom of an existing file.  // ctrl + C to cancel opertaion
:-> cat: f1.txt f2.txt > merged.txt: Combines files end-to-end into a new file.
:-> less: filename: Opens the file in a scrollable view.
:-> head: -n 10 filename: Displays only the first 10 lines of a file.Change the number to preview any size header.
:-> tail: -n 10 filename: Displays only the last 10 lines of a file.Useful for reading the end of logs.
:-> paste f1.txt f2.txt: Merges lines side-by-side using tabs as delimiters.
:-> join f1.txt f2.txt:  Merges lines based on a shared common column.
:-> sort filename: Alphabetizes all text lines from A to Z.
:-> sort -r filename: Sorts text lines in reverse alphabetical order.
:-> sort -n filename: Sorts numbers logically by their numerical value.
:-> sort -u filename: Sorts the file and removes duplicate lines.
:-> sort -m f1.txt f2.txt: Merges pre-sorted files together very quickly.
:-> sort -k 2 filenameSorts: the file based on the second column alphabetically

		// File Permissions & Execution Commands
:-> chmod: Changes the permissions of a file or directory.
:-> chmod +x monitor.sh: Adds execute permission to the monitor.sh Bash script.
:-> chmod -x monitor.sh: Removes execute permission from the monitor.sh Bash script.
:-> chmod 755 monitor.sh: Gives the owner read/write/execute permission and others read/execute permission.
:-> ./monitor.sh: Executes the monitor.sh script from the current directory.
:-> bash monitor.sh: Executes a Bash script using the Bash interpreter without requiring execute permission.


		// Process Monitoring Commands
:-> ps: Displays a snapshot of currently running processes.
:-> ps aux: Displays detailed information about processes from all users.
:-> ps aux --sort=-%cpu: Sorts processes by CPU usage in descending order.
:-> ps aux --sort=-%mem: Sorts processes by memory usage in descending order.
:-> top: Displays running processes and system resource usage.
:-> top -bn1: Runs top in batch mode and takes one monitoring snapshot.
:-> pkill: Sends a signal to processes matching a specified name.
:-> pkill yes: Terminates running processes named yes.


		// Memory Monitoring Commands
:-> free: Displays information about system RAM and swap memory.
:-> free -h: Displays memory information in human-readable units such as MB or GB.


		// Disk Monitoring Commands
:-> df: Displays available and used disk space of filesystems.
:-> df /: Displays disk-space usage for the filesystem containing the root directory.
:-> df -h: Displays disk usage in human-readable units such as MB or GB.


		// Text Processing Commands
:-> grep: Searches for lines containing a specified pattern or text.
:-> grep "Cpu(s)": Searches command output for the CPU information line.
:-> awk: Processes and extracts structured text and can perform calculations.
:-> awk '/Mem:/ {print $3}': Finds the Mem line and prints the third field.
:-> awk '{print $1}': Prints the first field/column from each input line.
:-> tr: Translates or deletes characters from input.
:-> tr -d '%': Removes the percentage (%) character from the input.


		// Pipe and Redirection
:-> |: Pipe operator; sends the output of one command as input to another command.
:-> top -bn1 | grep "Cpu(s)": Sends top output to grep to extract the CPU information line.
:-> ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -6: Sends sorted process output to head to display only the first 6 lines.
:-> >: Redirects command output to a file and overwrites the existing contents.
:-> >>: Redirects command output to a file and appends it to the existing contents.
:-> echo "text" > file.txt: Writes text to a file and replaces its previous contents.
:-> echo "text" >> file.txt: Adds text to the end of an existing file.


		// Shell Script Configuration Commands
:-> source: Loads and executes commands/variables from another shell script in the current shell.
:-> source config.sh: Loads configuration variables such as CPU_THRESHOLD into the current script.
:-> . config.sh: Short form of source; loads another shell script into the current shell.
:-> export: Makes a shell variable available to child processes.
:-> env: Displays environment variables available to the current process.


		// Bash Script Variables
:-> variable=value: Creates/assigns a value to a Bash variable.
:-> $variable: Accesses the value stored inside a Bash variable.
:-> ${variable}: Expands a variable value and is useful when separating the variable name from surrounding text.
:-> $(command): Command substitution; executes a command and stores/uses its output as a value.
:-> ${cpu_usage%.*}: Removes the shortest matching . * pattern from the end of the variable value.


		// Conditional Commands / Operators
:-> if: Executes commands when a specified condition is true.
:-> then: Marks the beginning of commands executed when the if condition is true.
:-> else: Executes alternative commands when the if condition is false.
:-> fi: Marks the end of an if statement.
:-> [ condition ]: Performs a conditional test in Bash.
:-> -gt: Tests whether one integer is greater than another.
:-> -lt: Tests whether one integer is less than another.
:-> -eq: Tests whether two integers are equal.
:-> -ne: Tests whether two integers are not equal.
:-> -ge: Tests whether one integer is greater than or equal to another.
:-> -le: Tests whether one integer is less than or equal to another.
:-> -f filename: Tests whether a specified path exists and is a regular file.


		// Bash Control Flow Commands
:-> while: Repeatedly executes a block of commands while a condition remains true.
:-> while true: Creates a loop that continues indefinitely until explicitly terminated.
:-> do: Marks the beginning of the commands inside a loop.
:-> done: Marks the end of a loop.
:-> case: Selects and executes a block of commands based on a matching value.
:-> esac: Marks the end of a Bash case statement.
:-> ;;: Terminates an individual case pattern.
:-> read -p: Displays a prompt and reads user input into a variable.


		// Bash Functions
:-> function_name(): Defines a Bash function.
:-> function_name: Calls/executes a previously defined Bash function.
:-> return: Exits a function and can optionally provide a return status.


		// Script Interpreter / Shebang
:-> #!/bin/bash: Shebang line that specifies Bash as the interpreter for the script.
:-> bash: Shell/interpreter used to execute Bash scripts and commands.


		// Log Management Commands
:-> tail -20 monitor.log: Displays the last 20 lines of the monitoring log.
:-> tail -10 monitor.log: Displays the last 10 lines of the monitoring log.
:-> echo "$(date) | CPU: $cpu_usage%" >> monitor.log: Adds a timestamped CPU monitoring entry to the log file.


		// Cron / Job Scheduling Commands
:-> crontab -e: Opens the current user's crontab for editing scheduled jobs.
:-> crontab -l: Lists the scheduled cron jobs of the current user.
:-> service cron status: Displays the current status of the cron service.
:-> systemctl status cron: Displays the status of the cron service on systems using systemd.
:-> */5 * * * * command: Cron expression that executes the specified command every 5 minutes.


		// Cron Expression Fields
:-> Minute: First field of a cron expression; specifies the minute of execution.
:-> Hour: Second field of a cron expression; specifies the hour of execution.
:-> Day of Month: Third field; specifies the day of the month.
:-> Month: Fourth field; specifies the month.
:-> Day of Week: Fifth field; specifies the day of the week.
:-> */5: Means every 5 units; in the minute field it means every 5 minutes.
:-> *: Means every possible value for that cron field.


		// ZIP / Project Deployment Commands
:-> unzip linux-server-monitor.zip: Extracts the Linux Server Monitor project from the ZIP archive.
:-> unzip -l linux-server-monitor.zip: Lists the files inside a ZIP archive without extracting them.
:-> mkdir linux-monitor-test: Creates a separate directory for testing the extracted project.
:-> find /mnt/k -name "linux-server-monitor.zip": Searches for the ZIP file inside the specified directory.


